---
import ToolPage from '../../components/ToolPage.astro';
import ContentTable from '../../components/ContentTable.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { ex, P } from '../../lib/tables';
import { sdlMonth } from '../../lib/engine/sg';
import { SRC } from '../../lib/sources';
import { formatMoney, formatPercent } from '../../lib/format';
const lang = 'en';
const e = ex(6500, { bonus: 6500 });
const rows = [3000, 4500, 6500, 8000, 12000].map((m) => { const r = ex(m, { bonus: m }); return [formatMoney(m), formatMoney(r.annual.gross), formatMoney(r.annual.cpfEmployer), formatMoney(r.annual.sdl, 2), formatMoney(r.annual.employerCost), formatPercent(r.annual.employerCost / r.annual.gross - 1)]; });
const faqs = [
  { q: 'How much does it cost to hire someone at $6,500 a month in Singapore?', a: `With a one-month bonus, about ${formatMoney(e.annual.employerCost)} a year for a citizen aged 30: ${formatMoney(e.annual.gross)} of salary and bonus, ${formatMoney(e.annual.cpfEmployer)} of employer CPF and ${formatMoney(e.annual.sdl, 2)} of Skills Development Levy. Add insurance, training and any foreign worker levy, which depend on your company and are not included here.` },
  { q: 'Is the Skills Development Levy paid on foreign employees?', a: `Yes. SDL is due for every employee working in Singapore, whatever the nationality or pass, at 0.25 % of the first ${formatMoney(P.sdl.wage_cap)} of monthly wages, with a minimum of ${formatMoney(P.sdl.min, 2)} and a maximum of ${formatMoney(P.sdl.max, 2)}. It is paid by the employer with the CPF submission and funds SkillsFuture training schemes.` },
  { q: 'Why does the employer cost percentage fall for higher salaries?', a: `Because employer CPF stops at the $8,000 ordinary wage ceiling and SDL stops at ${formatMoney(P.sdl.max, 2)} a month. Above those points each extra dollar of salary costs the employer one dollar. At ${formatMoney(3000)} a month the employer adds about 17 %; at ${formatMoney(12000)} the addition falls to about ${formatPercent(ex(12000, { bonus: 12000 }).annual.employerCost / ex(12000, { bonus: 12000 }).annual.gross - 1, 0)} of gross pay.` },
];
---
<ToolPage id="employerCost" title="Employer Cost Calculator Singapore 2026: CPF, SDL, Bonus" h1="Employer cost calculator: the full price of a salary in Singapore"
  description="Annual cost of an employee in Singapore for 2026: salary, bonus, employer CPF under the right rate table and the Skills Development Levy, from a monthly pay."
  intro="Budget a hire or compare two offers: the calculator adds employer CPF, with the bonus treated as additional wages, and the Skills Development Levy to the salary you pay."
  mode="employerCost" initialMonthly={6500} initialBonus={6500} faqs={faqs} related={['employerCpf', 'contribution', 'bonus', 'awCeiling', 'hourly', 'home']}
  sources={[SRC.cpf_rates, SRC.sdl, SRC.cpf_2027]}>
  <p>The salary on an offer letter is not what an employee costs. On {formatMoney(6500)} a month with a one-month bonus, a Singapore employer spends {formatMoney(e.annual.employerCost)} a year for a citizen aged 30: {formatMoney(e.annual.gross)} of pay, {formatMoney(e.annual.cpfEmployer)} of employer CPF, and {formatMoney(e.annual.sdl, 2)} of Skills Development Levy. The calculator computes each piece with the official rules rather than a flat percentage. Employer CPF uses the rate table that matches the employee’s status and age, stops at the {formatMoney(P.cpf.ow_ceiling_month)} ordinary wage ceiling, and treats the bonus as additional wages capped by the Additional Wage ceiling. SDL is worked out month by month at 0.25 % of the first {formatMoney(P.sdl.wage_cap)}, with its {formatMoney(P.sdl.min, 2)} floor and {formatMoney(P.sdl.max, 2)} cap, including the bonus month. For a foreign employee, CPF drops out and only SDL remains, which is why the same salary costs less to an employer when the hire holds an Employment Pass.</p>
  <h2>Annual cost with a one-month bonus, citizen aged 30</h2>
  <ContentTable headers={['Monthly salary', 'Salary + bonus', 'Employer CPF', 'SDL', 'Total cost', 'On top of pay']} align={['r', 'r', 'r', 'r', 'r', 'r']} rows={rows} />
  <p>SDL on a single month varies from {formatMoney(sdlMonth(700), 2)} for a part-timer on $700 to {formatMoney(sdlMonth(3000), 2)} on {formatMoney(3000)} and {formatMoney(sdlMonth(8000), 2)} from {formatMoney(P.sdl.wage_cap)} upwards. Small as it is, it is due on every employee, and payroll systems that forget the minimum on low wages are a frequent source of CPF Board reminders.</p>
  <h2>Costs this calculator leaves out</h2>
  <p>Several costs depend on the company rather than on the salary and are not modelled: the foreign worker levy for S Pass and Work Permit holders, which varies by sector and quota tier; work injury compensation insurance, compulsory for manual workers and for non-manual employees earning up to $2,600 a month; medical and group insurance; and transport or housing benefits. Government support such as the Senior Employment Credit or wage offsets, when available, reduces the cost after the fact. Our <a href={route('employerCpf', lang)}>employer CPF calculator</a> shows the contribution alone, month by month.</p>
</ToolPage>
